CONTENT = {
'read-record': '''
<section id="record-result"><h2>Result and prerequisites</h2><p>You will prepare a short reading note that identifies a record’s exact identity, selected format, content integrity, standing, scope and claim limits. Have the record, selected contract and applicable registry or decision evidence available. A filename or diagram label cannot supply those facts.</p><p>This is a manual inspection of the materials. The documentation offers examples and reference contracts; it does not query a live registry.</p></section>
<section id="record-select"><h2>1. Select the record and its contract</h2><p>Open the <a href="record-examples.html#example-work">complete Work example</a>. Record its <code>node_id</code> and <code>schema_version</code>. In the <a href="lab-reference-library.html#type-cal-artifact-type-work-v1">Work reference</a>, select the exact format version and its required fields. Keep the source identity and digest alongside the reading note.</p><p>A <code>node_id</code> is not necessarily a version field. Use the selected storage/versioning contract to identify the exact content; do not infer version or standing from its spelling.</p></section>
<section id="record-standing"><h2>2. Establish standing independently</h2><p>The example says <code>lifecycle_state: candidate</code> and <code>authority_effect: none</code>. No owner acceptance is supplied. Write “candidate documentation specimen” in the note. For real material, check the applicable standing selection and exact owner decision; an admitted Artifact Type does not admit every instance of that type.</p><p>The reference library presents the standing selected in its captured source material. A source file can retain its authored candidate wording even when a later decision governs its effective standing. Preserve both facts and identify which selection is applicable.</p></section>
<section id="record-boundaries"><h2>3. Read scope, evidence and connections</h2><p>Check the owner, scope, inputs, outputs, evidence references and claim ceiling (the strongest conclusion its scope and evidence support). Open the referenced material before treating it as support. For each connection, inspect its direction, ordered endpoint kinds and required qualifiers in the declared relation rule. A local <code>relation_refs</code> entry does not contain the complete relation assertion.</p><p>For Northstar, Work belongs to the repair Project. It does not authorize deployment, establish a repair or close the incident. The <a href="record-examples.html#connection-examples">connection examples</a> show the required membership qualifier.</p></section>
<section id="record-output"><h2>Expected output and next step</h2><dl class="walk-step"><div><dt>Identity and format</dt><dd>Work specimen, exact saved JSON; Work/v1 selected.</dd></div><div><dt>Standing</dt><dd>Candidate documentation material; no acceptance decision supplied.</dd></div><div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>S1 / Northstar / status=active / E12 identifier coverage.</dd></div><div><dt>Permitted conclusion</dt><dd>The record describes investigation Work and its expected outputs. Its existence does not establish success or permission.</dd></div><div><dt>Next route</dt><dd>Use the accountable owner’s review route for proposed acceptance or changed effects.</dd></div></dl><p><strong>Common failures:</strong> reading the latest-looking filename as the selected version; treating a schema pass as acceptance; relying on unresolved evidence links; reading a view as a competing authority source.</p><p><a href="prepare-incident-assessment.html">Next: prepare an incident assessment →</a></p></section>''',
'prepare-incident-assessment': '''
<section id="assessment-result"><h2>Result and prerequisites</h2><p>Prepare a candidate assessment of the omission in the Northstar export. Your output will state what was observed, which material supports it, and what remains unresolved. Use the <a href="incident-walkthrough.html">incident walkthrough</a> for background.</p><p>Have the saved input, output, criterion, method, comparison receipt and applicable authority envelope (the evidence for its scoped permission). Before reading customer material or saving outputs, verify a usable task view and permission for those actions. The steps below use invented local files and a manual preparation route.</p></section>
<section id="assessment-inputs"><h2>1. Fix the question and inputs</h2><p>Ask: “Does E12 contain every required identifier from S1 for Northstar with status=active?” Apply the <a href="record-examples.html#example-definition">identifier-completeness criterion</a>. Keep snapshot, account, filters and output in the assessment; omitting any of them changes the question.</p><dl class="walk-step"><div><dt>Input</dt><dd><a href="examples/S1.json">S1</a>: fixed snapshot; Northstar; status=active; required identifiers 1–10,000.</dd></div><div><dt>Output</dt><dd><a href="examples/E12.json">E12</a>: saved export from the declared scope; identifiers 1–9,800.</dd></div><div><dt>Method</dt><dd>Version 2: form the set of required IDs absent from the saved output.</dd></div><div><dt>Permission</dt><dd>The incident’s scoped 09:45–10:15 delegation permits saved-input inspection, identifier comparison and candidate preparation through its recording route. Assignment alone supplies no permission.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section id="assessment-compare"><h2>2. Preserve the comparison</h2><p>Compare identifiers, then save the exact missing set and the input/output digests. Counts alone cannot show which required rows are absent: an export could have the same number of rows with different identifiers.</p><p>The <a href="examples/comparison.json">worked receipt</a> lists identifiers 9,801–10,000 as missing. Its digests identify the actual local teaching files. Preserve this datum in the Operational Observation. The selected Measurement Result carrier is specialized for residue entropy; the <a href="record-examples.html#example-measurement-result">separate measurement example</a> explains that boundary. No entropy or causal claim follows from the omission count.</p></section>
<section id="assessment-records"><h2>3. Prepare the required records</h2><ol class="task-steps"><li><strong>Operational Observation:</strong> record the datum, time, method, action and evidence; keep interpretation bounded.</li><li><strong>Evidence Set:</strong> group the selected sources and comparison, with coverage and limitations.</li><li><strong>Failure Occurrence:</strong> connect the observed omission to the expected criterion.</li><li><strong>Classification Assignment:</strong> identify the manifestation under a selected scheme; do not replace a causal investigation with a category.</li><li><strong>Failure Assessment:</strong> state occurrence, mode, cause, impact, containment, recovery and uncertainty separately.</li></ol><p>Open the <a href="record-examples.html">complete examples</a> for these formats. They include every schema-required field and a one-field failure. For real material, validate the exact selected schema, resolve references and check the ordered relation contracts as separate obligations.</p></section>
<section id="assessment-output"><h2>Expected output: a bounded assessment</h2><dl class="walk-step"><div><dt>Occurrence</dt><dd>200 required identifiers are absent from E12 in the fixed S1 / Northstar / status=active scope.</dd></div><div><dt>Mode</dt><dd>Candidate manifestation classification: missing required identifiers.</dd></div><div><dt>Cause</dt><dd>Unresolved. Pagination may be investigated as a hypothesis; it is not established by this comparison.</dd></div><div><dt>Impact</dt><dd>Output omission established. Downstream uses and other accounts unresolved.</dd></div><div><dt>Containment and recovery</dt><dd>Neither established by the saved comparison.</dd></div><div><dt>Standing</dt><dd>Candidate assessment awaiting its applicable review.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section id="assessment-review"><h2>4. Send material to the applicable review</h2><p>Package exact record identities, selected versions, evidence digests, the comparison and open questions for the service owner’s review route. The owner can request more evidence or decide a scoped next action. Investigation completion, assessment acceptance, code acceptance, deployment, recovery and incident closure have separate conditions.</p><p><strong>Common failures:</strong> using counts instead of IDs; losing account or filters; calling a suspected mechanism the cause; saying revision 8 is deployed; calling the incident closed because the proposal was saved.</p><p>If the task view has expired, <a href="resume-agent-work.html">prepare a fresh continuation</a> before further agent action. The saved evidence remains inspectable; it does not extend permission or view usability.</p></section>''',
'prepare-definition': '''
<section id="definition-result"><h2>Result and prerequisites</h2><p>Prepare one exact candidate meaning and the materials its owner needs to review it. Select the applicable Definition contract, semantic owner, authority scope, supporting sources, primary consumers, index/registry route and drift route. The <a href="record-examples.html#example-definition">complete criterion specimen</a> uses Definition/v2.</p><p>The specimen passes that selected schema, but its source posture and acceptance obligations remain unresolved. Use it to inspect structure; prepare real source and owner evidence independently.</p></section>
<section id="definition-meaning"><h2>1. State the meaning and its boundary</h2><p>Identify the term, stable identity and proposed version. State one governing (normative) meaning, its applicability, exclusions and boundaries. For export identifier completeness, declare S, A, F and E, then require every identifier in RequiredIds(S,A,F) to appear in ExportedIds(E).</p><p>Value accuracy, live-source consistency, access permission and incident recovery each fall outside that criterion. A case with all IDs present but incorrect values exposes this boundary. It does not falsify the identifier-coverage statement.</p></section>
<section id="definition-voices"><h2>2. Align the three voices</h2><p>The scientific/formal voice states the governing meaning. The plain-language and domain-context voices are explicitly non-normative explanations. Use the same scope in each voice. Avoid making “every customer record everywhere” the plain-language explanation of a statement about one fixed account, filter set and snapshot.</p><p>Declare testable conditions and notation. Preserve an example that satisfies the criterion and an example that fails it, with the selected inputs visible.</p></section>
<section id="definition-review-material"><h2>3. Prepare the review obligations</h2><ol class="task-steps"><li>Bind source identities, selectors, digests and admitted source posture using the applicable source contract.</li><li>Identify primary consumers and any affected meanings, formats or connections.</li><li>Prepare the registry postimage (the proposed registry contents after the change) and synchronized index entry; preserve the exact canonical JSON and generated projection relationship.</li><li>Declare the current drift audit, accountable owner and challenge route.</li><li>Inspect internal consistency and non-collapse: meaning must remain separate from representation, classification, permission and lifecycle standing.</li></ol><p>Schema checks establish required representation. Reference closure, source posture, generated projection consistency, the registry/index and semantic review remain separate checks.</p></section>
<section id="definition-output"><h2>Expected output and owner decision</h2><p>Submit the exact candidate version with the formal meaning, explanatory voices, boundaries, source evidence, affected consumers, proposed index/registry changes and review findings. Keep open obligations visible. Only the applicable exact owner decision can make that version governing authority within its declared scope.</p><p><strong>Common failures:</strong> treating a schema pass as acceptance; hiding changed meaning as a formatting update; allowing the plain-language voice to expand the formal scope; inferring agent permission from definition acceptance.</p><p>A changed meaning needs its applicable semantic change route. After acceptance, update affected consumers and derived views through their own rules. <a href="governance-lab-reference.html#change">Read the lifecycle distinctions →</a></p></section>''',
'resume-agent-work': '''
<section id="resume-result"><h2>Result and prerequisites</h2><p>Prepare a usable continuation for an interrupted investigation. You need the saved Project State, exact assignment, current authority evidence, applicable policies, view request and selected freshness/expiry rules. Recovery of project knowledge and permission to act must both be checked.</p><p>At 10:10 UTC, Northstar task view 3 expires. The 09:45–10:15 delegation may still be current, but the expired view prevents continued reliance on that task context.</p></section>
<section id="resume-pause"><h2>1. Pause and preserve permitted outputs</h2><p>Record why the view is unusable and where the agent stopped. Persist only outputs permitted by the recording route, preserving their candidate standing and source references. Do not use temporary session notes as an accepted project conclusion.</p><p>If the same session remains active, refreshing its views does not itself renew or expand authority. If the session terminates, its machine instance and short memory end with it.</p></section>
<section id="resume-reconstruct"><h2>2. Select durable project knowledge</h2><p>For a later session, select saved Project State: Work, comparison receipt, observations, assessment material, decisions and unresolved questions. Generate or obtain newly checked Context and Ontology Views under their applicable request, selection and freshness contracts.</p><p>The <a href="record-examples.html#example-context-view">Context View example</a> identifies included material by selector and digest, records exclusions and sets expiry. A structurally valid JSON view can still be unusable because its source or time conditions changed.</p></section>
<section id="resume-authority"><h2>3. Recheck the authority envelope</h2><p>Read the exact current assignment and delegation separately. Check eligible agent, scope, action kinds, policies, approval gates, period, revocation and budgets. At or after 10:15 UTC the old delegation cannot support later actions. A new session may need a new or renewed grant; copying the old context does not transfer permission.</p><p><a href="record-examples.html#example-agent-assignment">Assignment</a> describes expected participation. <a href="record-examples.html#example-delegation">Delegation</a> supplies its scoped grant only when its governing evidence is established. Neither a view nor an agent’s recollection grants authority.</p></section>
<section id="resume-session"><h2>4. Bind the continuation and its stop conditions</h2><p>Create the applicable session envelope for the selected concrete instance, assignment, exact views, policies, authority, inputs, budgets and cursor. For a new session, use a new ephemeral machine instance and session-owned working memory. The <a href="record-examples.html#example-agent-session">Agent Session specimen</a> shows the required fields.</p><p>Stop again if permission is revoked or expires, a view becomes unusable, the budget is exhausted or an approval gate is reached. Preserve the previous session’s records as history; do not silently resume its temporary memory.</p></section>
<section id="resume-output"><h2>Expected output and next action</h2><dl class="walk-step"><div><dt>Durable basis</dt><dd>Exact saved evidence and candidate assessment, with cause, wider impact and recovery still unresolved.</dd></div><div><dt>Task knowledge</dt><dd>Newly checked views with sources, limits and usable freshness/expiry conditions.</dd></div><div><dt>Permission</dt><dd>Current scoped authority for the intended action; no inferred extension from the previous session.</dd></div><div><dt>Continuation</dt><dd>Applicable session envelope and stop conditions, with a new instance when starting a new session.</dd></div></dl><p><strong>Common failures:</strong> resuming on the expired view; treating saved state as permission; giving a machine instance durable private memory; calling an assessment accepted because it survived a session.</p><p><a href="governance-lab-reference.html#agent">Read how agent work and project knowledge fit together →</a></p></section>''',
}


def render(root):
    shell=(root/'templates/start-here.html.in').read_text()
    outputs={}
    for key,(title,description) in PAGES.items():
        content = example_content() if key=='record-examples' else '<header class="start-masthead"><p class="docs-breadcrumb">CyberAlchemy Lab / Practical guides</p><h1>'+escape(title)+'</h1><p class="start-deck">'+escape(description)+'</p></header>'+CONTENT[key]
        document=Document(content)
        toc=''.join('<a href="#'+n.attrs['id']+'">'+escape(n.first(lambda h:h.tag=='h2').text())+'</a>' for n in document.root.all(lambda n:n.tag=='section' and n.attrs.get('id')))
        page=shell.replace('<title>Start here · CyberAlchemy Lab</title>','<title>'+title+' · CyberAlchemy Lab</title>')
        page=re.sub(r'<meta name="description" content="[^"]*">','<meta name="description" content="'+escape(description)+'">',page)
        page=page.replace('href="#start-here"','href="#'+key+'"').replace('Skip to the introduction','Skip to the guide').replace('data-site-start aria-current="page"','data-site-start')
        page=re.sub(r'<article id="start-here".*?</article>',lambda m:'<article id="'+key+'" class="start-content" tabindex="-1">'+content+'</article>',page,flags=re.S)
        page=re.sub(r'(<details class="docs-toc" id="start-toc"><summary>On this page</summary><nav aria-label="Headings on this page">).*?(</nav></details>)',lambda m:m[1]+toc+m[2],page,flags=re.S)
        page=page.replace('{{READING_TIME}}','8')
        assert '{{' not in page
        outputs[key+'.html']=page
    return outputs
